### ============================================
### WEBSERV: FILE DELETE HANDLER TEST SUITE
### ============================================
### Description:
###   Tests the server's ability to handle DELETE requests for file removal.
###
### Pre-requisites:
###   1. A running server with the 'delete_server.conf' configuration.
###   2. The test environment created by the 'setup_delete_env.sh' script.
###      This script must be run before each full test run to ensure files exist.
### ============================================

### Variables
@host = localhost
@port = 8080
@baseUrl = http://{{host}}:{{port}}
@userAgent = WebServ-Test-Client/1.0

### =================================================================
### Section 1: Successful Deletion (2xx Responses)
### =================================================================

### Test 1.1: Delete an existing, accessible file
# The most basic success case.
# EXPECTS: 204 No Content. The response body must be empty.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/deletable_file.txt
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###

### Test 1.2: Delete a read-only file in a writable directory
# Tests if the server correctly relies on OS-level permissions.
# The OS allows deleting a read-only file if the parent directory is writable.
# EXPECTS: 204 No Content.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/protected_file.txt
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

### =================================================================
### Section 2: Client Error Handling (4xx Responses)
### =================================================================

### Test 2.1: File Not Found
# Attempting to delete a file that does not exist.
# EXPECTS: 404 Not Found.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/non_existent_file.txt
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###

### Test 2.2: Attempt to Delete a Directory
# The handler should only delete files, not directories.
# EXPECTS: 409 Conflict (or 403 Forbidden). 409 is more specific.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/a_directory/
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###

### Test 2.3: No Write Permission on Parent Directory
# The server process cannot delete a file if it lacks write/execute
# permissions on the containing directory.
# EXPECTS: 403 Forbidden.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/no_parent_write_perms/unreachable.txt
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###

### Test 2.4: Path Traversal Attempt
# Attempting to delete a file outside of the configured root.
# The server should sanitize the path and not allow this.
# EXPECTS: 400 Bad Request (or 404 Not Found if path is just normalized).
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/delete/../../../../etc/hosts
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###

### Test 2.5: Method Not Allowed
# Sending a GET request to a location that only allows DELETE.
# EXPECTS: 405 Method Not Allowed.
# The response must include an "Allow: DELETE" header.
GET {{baseUrl}}/delete/deletable_file.txt
Host: example.com
User-Agent: {{userAgent}}

###